WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.689
Welcome. This short video
introduces ideas and tools for

2
00:00:04.689 --> 00:00:08.689
creating accessible research
and educational material.

3
00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:14.103
The goal is practical: making research
and teaching material easier to create,

4
00:00:14.103 --> 00:00:17.696
easier to understand, and
easier to revisit later.

5
00:00:17.896 --> 00:00:20.418
This presentation is also its own example.

6
00:00:20.418 --> 00:00:24.201
The same slides and speaker notes
that support a live talk were

7
00:00:24.201 --> 00:00:27.933
used to generate this narrated
video with an AI assistant.

8
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:32.214
The first tool is a
LaTeX formula generator.

9
00:00:32.414 --> 00:00:37.831
Instead of remembering LaTeX syntax, you
describe the equation in ordinary language.

10
00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:42.309
Here, the prompt at the bottom left
asks for the integral of f of x,

11
00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:45.641
where f of x is x to the
fourth power plus three.

12
00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:49.766
The assistant inserts the matching
LaTeX code into the document,

13
00:00:49.766 --> 00:00:52.837
and you can choose to
keep or undo the change.

14
00:00:53.037 --> 00:00:55.671
On the right is the compiled result:

15
00:00:55.671 --> 00:00:59.989
f of x equals x to the fourth
plus three, and its integral,

16
00:00:59.989 --> 00:01:03.678
x to the fifth over five,
plus three x, plus C.

17
00:01:03.878 --> 00:01:06.263
This example uses OpenAI's Prism,

18
00:01:06.263 --> 00:01:11.790
which will be replaced in January 2027
by similar functionality in Codex.

19
00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:16.595
One important point: the AI does the
typing, but the researcher still checks the

20
00:01:16.595 --> 00:01:20.472
mathematics and makes sure the
formula means what was intended.

21
00:01:21.322 --> 00:01:26.251
Next: creating videos with Claude,
anchored in presentation slides.

22
00:01:26.451 --> 00:01:29.400
Start by preparing your own
slides with the material

23
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.066
and subtopics you
deliberately want to present.

24
00:01:32.066 --> 00:01:36.627
They establish the scientific content
and the story the video should follow.

25
00:01:36.827 --> 00:01:39.889
Writing your own narrative
anchors the AI agent,

26
00:01:39.889 --> 00:01:43.521
keeps it on topic, and helps
to avoid hallucinations.

27
00:01:43.721 --> 00:01:48.110
Then add speaker notes. They hold
the explanation behind each slide,

28
00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:51.466
for you as the presenter
and as guidance for the AI.

29
00:01:51.466 --> 00:01:54.814
The notes behind this
very video are an example.

30
00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:57.513
For narration, you can
prompt the AI to use

31
00:01:57.513 --> 00:02:00.128
an open-source voice
model, as in this video,

32
00:02:00.128 --> 00:02:05.028
or a service such as ElevenLabs for a
more natural voice with better intonation.

33
00:02:05.228 --> 00:02:09.937
That raises a question worth discussing:
should we clone our own voices?

34
00:02:10.137 --> 00:02:13.319
You can also ask for subtitles
in different languages,

35
00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:15.750
for more inclusion and accessibility.

36
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.954
On the right are example videos
made this way, on AlphaFold 2,

37
00:02:19.954 --> 00:02:23.886
diffusion, and tokenization,
available at the link below.

38
00:02:24.086 --> 00:02:26.820
Whatever tools you use,
review the generated

39
00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.176
explanation and captions
before sharing the video.

40
00:02:31.026 --> 00:02:35.479
The third idea: using video
transcripts for references.

41
00:02:35.679 --> 00:02:38.399
Long recorded lectures,
like this one on machine

42
00:02:38.399 --> 00:02:40.780
learning for protein structure prediction,

43
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.580
are hard to search by
scrubbing through them.

44
00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.322
On YouTube, you can
open the transcript with

45
00:02:46.322 --> 00:02:49.057
the Show transcript
button below the video.

46
00:02:49.257 --> 00:02:53.919
It opens a panel where every line of
spoken text is linked to a timestamp.

47
00:02:54.119 --> 00:02:57.652
Give an AI assistant the video's
URL and ask it to read the

48
00:02:57.652 --> 00:03:01.364
transcript and return the timestamp
of a specific explanation.

49
00:03:01.364 --> 00:03:04.249
You can also supply the
transcript directly.

50
00:03:04.449 --> 00:03:06.981
Automatic transcripts can contain errors,

51
00:03:06.981 --> 00:03:12.048
so watch the actual passage and check
its context before citing or sharing it.

52
00:03:12.898 --> 00:03:16.178
The fourth idea is interactive graphs.

53
00:03:16.378 --> 00:03:19.653
When many curves share one
plot, as in the lower chart,

54
00:03:19.653 --> 00:03:22.166
following any single one is difficult.

55
00:03:22.366 --> 00:03:25.588
Both charts show ROC curves
per forecast lead time,

56
00:03:25.588 --> 00:03:28.304
comparing a neural
network with a baseline.

57
00:03:28.304 --> 00:03:31.021
In the upper chart,
one scenario stands out

58
00:03:31.021 --> 00:03:33.924
while the others fade into the background.

59
00:03:34.124 --> 00:03:37.781
That is because a forecast scenario
was selected in the legend,

60
00:03:37.781 --> 00:03:40.063
which makes its graph more salient.

61
00:03:40.263 --> 00:03:44.054
This is possible because Weights
and Biases supports Vega-Lite,

62
00:03:44.054 --> 00:03:47.433
a language for customizing
charts for accessibility.

63
00:03:47.633 --> 00:03:50.729
With it, you can adjust
colours, labels, and legends,

64
00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:55.243
and add interactive highlighting when
hovering over or clicking on lines.

65
00:03:55.443 --> 00:03:58.754
Choices like these make
comparisons easier to follow,

66
00:03:58.754 --> 00:04:01.940
especially when many
results are shown at once.

67
00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.221
Paul will show you more about the general

68
00:04:04.221 --> 00:04:07.161
capabilities of Weights and
Biases at the next booth.

69
00:04:08.011 --> 00:04:13.242
The last idea: intuitive
visualization and animation libraries.

70
00:04:13.442 --> 00:04:18.580
Animation can make a mathematical
process easier to follow, step by step.

71
00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:23.273
At the top is smalldiffusion, a
library focused on diffusion models.

72
00:04:23.273 --> 00:04:27.436
These panels show how its output
changes as the guidance scale,

73
00:04:27.436 --> 00:04:30.593
labelled CFG scale,
goes from zero to three.

74
00:04:30.793 --> 00:04:34.861
Below is Manim, a more general
mathematical animation engine,

75
00:04:34.861 --> 00:04:39.379
shown here with a curve, a secant
line, and the area beneath it.

76
00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:42.266
Because the animation
is generated from code,

77
00:04:42.266 --> 00:04:45.311
libraries like these help
reduce the hallucinations

78
00:04:45.311 --> 00:04:48.606
that often come from purely
video-based generators.

79
00:04:48.806 --> 00:04:51.591
You can give them to an
AI agent as dependencies,

80
00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.513
so it can build the visual
explanation for you.

81
00:04:54.713 --> 00:04:57.605
As always, inspect the
code and check that the

82
00:04:57.605 --> 00:05:00.559
animation represents
the concept correctly.

83
00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:03.888
Before you move on, think
of one concept from your own

84
00:05:03.888 --> 00:05:07.035
work that could benefit
from a visual explanation.

85
00:05:07.885 --> 00:05:11.490
That's all for this overview.
Thank you for watching.
